❓ Question 5 (Syllabus Topic 7.3 & 7.4: Guardrails & Fallback Strategies)
In your application code (src/App.jsx and server/index.js), how did you handle edge cases and system failures?

Frontend Validation: What happens if the user leaves the Project Name or Description blank and clicks Generate Project Plan?
Backend Fallback Strategy: What happens if the network drops or the Gemini AI API returns an error? How does your backend and frontend catch that error so the app doesn't crash?


==============ans================


🛡️ 1. Frontend Validation (Guardrails)
In your 
src/App.jsx
:

javascript


// Lines 17 - 20
if (!projectName.trim() || !description.trim()) {
  alert('Please fill in Project Name and Description.')
  return
}
What it does: If the user leaves the input fields blank, React stops the submission immediately. It doesn't send empty network requests to Express, saving server bandwidth and API quota.
Button Guardrail: Your button is also grayed out (disabled) until the user fills in the required fields!
🚨 2. Backend Fallback & Error Handling (try/catch)
In your 
server/index.js
:

javascript


// Lines 20 - 48
try {
  // Call Gemini AI
  const response = await ai.models.generateContent(...)
  res.json({ plan });
} catch (error) {
  console.error('AI Generation Error:', error);
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to generate plan from AI.' });
}
What it does: If Gemini AI is down, rate-limited, or fails, the try/catch block catches the error instead of letting Node.js crash. Express sends a clean 500 status with { error: "..." } back to React.
💻 3. Frontend Fallback Display (try/catch/finally)
In your 
src/App.jsx
:

javascript


try {
  // fetch request
} catch (error) {
  setResponse('Failed to connect to backend server. Make sure node index.js is running on port 5000.')
} finally {
  setLoading(false) // Always re-enables the button!
}
What it does: If the server fails or network disconnects, React catches the error and displays a friendly error message in the AI Response box. The finally block ensures the button stops saying "Generating..." and becomes clickable again!
🎙️ How to say it in an interview:
"We implement validation and error handling on both ends. On the frontend, we validate required inputs before triggering fetch(). On the backend, we wrap external LLM API calls in a try/catch block. If the AI service or network fails, the backend sends a 500 error payload, React catches it, displays a user-friendly error message, and the finally block resets the button loading state so the app never crashes or freezes."

