🎯 Next Interview Question — Question 4

“How do you structure the AI response so that it can be easily displayed in your React UI?”

//==============


I manage the input and output flow between the frontend, backend, and Gemini in multiple steps.

First, when the user enters the project details, React stores them in state. When the user clicks Generate Project Plan, I send those details to the backend using `fetch()` 
with a POST request. I use `JSON.stringify()` to convert the JavaScript object into a JSON string for the request body.

When Express receives the request, I have configured `app.use(express.json())`. This middleware parses the JSON request body, so `req.body` becomes a JavaScript object. I can 
then access the individual project details from `req.body`, such as `projectName`, `description`, `experience`, `technology`, and `deadline`.

These project details are then passed to my `generateMultiStepPlan()` function in the prompt service.

Inside the prompt service, I use a three-step chained AI workflow. In each step, I instruct Gemini to return the response in JSON format. I then use `JSON.parse()` to convert 
the JSON response into a JavaScript object.

The output from one step is used as relevant context for the next step. For example, the project overview and complexity generated in Step 1 are passed into Step 2 to generate 
development phases and tasks.

After all three steps are completed, I combine their outputs into one final `finalPlan` object. Before sending it to the frontend, the backend validates the structure using 
`validateProjectPlan()`.

Finally, the backend sends the validated plan as JSON to React using `res.json()`. React receives the response using `res.json()` on the frontend and stores the plan using 
`setPlan()`. The plan is then used to render the structured project information in the UI.


