🎯 Question 5

“Why did you choose to return the AI response as structured JSON instead of returning plain text?”

//=============
I chose structured JSON because my frontend needs to work with different parts of the AI response separately.

In my application, the project plan contains different fields such as `projectOverview`, `complexity`, `estimatedTotalDays`, `phases`, `risks`, and `testingPlan`.

If I returned plain text, I would have to manually parse that text to extract each section. With structured JSON, I can directly access each property and render it in the UI.

For example, I can use `plan.projectOverview` to display the overview and `plan.phases.map()` to display the development phases and tasks.

It also gives me a predictable response structure, which makes validation easier and makes the communication between my backend and frontend more reliable.

So, I use JSON because it provides a structured, predictable format that my React application can easily process and render.


//====================================================


What happens if Gemini returns an invalid or malformed response in your application? How do you handle it?”

//===============

If Gemini returns an invalid or malformed response, I handle it on the backend before sending it to the frontend.

First, because I expect Gemini to return JSON, I use `JSON.parse()` to convert the response into a JavaScript object. If the response is not valid JSON, `JSON.parse()` will 
throw an error, which is caught by my `try-catch` block and the server returns an error response.

After parsing, I also validate the structure of the final plan using my `validateProjectPlan()` function. It checks whether the plan is an object and whether required fields 
like `projectOverview`, `complexity`, and `phases` exist with the expected types.

If the validation fails, I return a `502` response with an error message instead of sending malformed data to the frontend.

This prevents my React UI from trying to render an unexpected AI response and gives me a controlled way to handle malformed AI output.

//=============================================================


“Why are you calling Gemini from the backend instead of directly from your React frontend?”

//================



I call Gemini from the backend instead of directly from the React frontend mainly for security and better control.

The most important reason is that my Gemini API key is a secret, so I should not expose it in the frontend code. In my application, I store the API key in the backend `.env` 
file and initialize the Gemini client in `server/index.js`.

The backend also gives me a central place to control the AI integration. I can build prompts, manage the three-step AI workflow, validate the AI response, handle errors, and
apply guardrails before sending the result to the frontend.

So the architecture is:

React frontend → Express backend → Gemini → Express backend → React frontend.

This keeps the API key protected and allows the backend to control and validate the AI input and output.\



